# 7. AI-POWERED COMPLAINT PROCESSING

Extend existing AI functionality instead of duplicating it.

### AI capabilities

1. Classify complaint descriptions into supported categories.
2. Suggest the relevant department.
3. Identify possible urgency and severity.
4. Detect potentially similar or duplicate complaints.
5. Suggest an initial response or resolution checklist.
6. Summarize long complaint descriptions for administrators.
7. Identify repeated problems and recurring campus issues.

### Important rules

- AI must produce validated structured output, not arbitrary instructions.
- Validate all AI output against an application-defined schema and allowed categories.
- Never trust an AI-generated department, user ID, role, status, or database query without backend validation.
- AI suggestions must not override authorization or safety policies.
- A simple deterministic fallback must work when the AI API is unavailable.
- Do not call the AI provider repeatedly for the same unchanged complaint without a reason.
- Add sensible timeouts, limited retries, rate limiting, and error handling.
- Keep API keys exclusively on the server.
- Never send passwords, JWT secrets, or unnecessary personal information to an AI provider.
- Treat complaint descriptions and uploaded documents as untrusted user content.
- Clearly distinguish an AI suggestion from an approved administrative decision.

### Duplicate detection

Use multiple signals:
- Text similarity
- Category
- Location
- Time window
- Related open complaints

A duplicate candidate must not automatically cause a complaint to be discarded.

Show possible matches to the user or administrator and allow a legitimate new complaint to proceed.

Where appropriate, link related complaints or group them under a common incident.

Measure duplicate-detection quality with test examples rather than claiming a fixed accuracy without evidence.

