# 14. SECURITY AND DATA PRIVACY

Implement production-minded security measures.

- Hash passwords using bcrypt/bcryptjs with an appropriate work factor.
- Keep JWT secrets, AI API keys, SMTP credentials, and database credentials in environment variables.
- Never commit `.env` files or secrets to Git.
- Validate and sanitize incoming data.
- Use authentication and role-based authorization middleware.
- Protect all admin endpoints.
- Prevent insecure direct object reference vulnerabilities.
- Check complaint ownership or assigned permissions on every relevant request.
- Rate-limit login, password reset, and sensitive endpoints.
- Use secure CORS allowlists for approved frontend origins.
- Configure appropriate security headers.
- Apply request size limits.
- Validate file uploads, file types, file sizes, and storage permissions.
- Do not expose stack traces or sensitive database details to users.
- Avoid logging passwords, access tokens, reset tokens, or secret values.
- Use secure token storage and expiration policies appropriate to the existing architecture.
- Use HTTPS in production.
- Restrict database access through MongoDB Atlas network access rules and database permissions.
- Keep audit logs for important administrative and security actions.

Use a secure file-storage solution for production evidence uploads. Do not assume that a local upload folder is durable in serverless or ephemeral hosting environments.

Never expose sensitive environment values through frontend `VITE_` variables.

